( '4.4', 'CASE 4.4.01', 'Legal Personality and Business Classification', 'Analyze how Figure 9 divides forms of business ownership between unincorporated and incorporated structures. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Unincorporated businesses are not legal entities of their own, whereas incorporated businesses are legal persons.', 'A partnership qualifies as an incorporated business because partners may contribute capital jointly.', 'Filing profits on personal income tax automatically makes a sole proprietorship a separate legal person.', 'Listing shares on a stock exchange is the only criterion that separates incorporated from unincorporated firms.', 'Because a limited liability company restricts owners'' liability, it must be classified as unincorporated.'], ARRAY[true, false, false, false, false], ARRAY['TRUE — The unincorporated/incorporated split turns on legal personality. Unincorporated firms (sole traders, partnerships) are not legal entities of their own; incorporated companies are legal persons. Pooling capital, filing personal tax, listing shares, or enjoying limited liability must be attached to the correct side of that split — limited liability marks incorporated forms, and listing is not the sole criterion of incorporation.

