( '4.4', 'CASE 4.4.30', 'Subtle Incorporation Distinctions', 'Analyze subtle distinctions students must apply when judging incorporated versus unincorporated status. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Paying corporate tax is evidence of incorporated status because unincorporated owners never face business taxation.', 'A partnership remains unincorporated even when a partnership deed sets formal management rules among partners.', 'An incorporated corporation remains a legal person if shareholders sell shares to new investors on the secondary market.', 'Sole traders remain unincorporated when the proprietor registers for value-added tax.', 'Directors may manage an incorporated company while shareholders remain passive capital providers.'], ARRAY[true, false, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — The unincorporated/incorporated split turns on legal personality. Unincorporated firms (sole traders, partnerships) are not legal entities of their own; incorporated companies are legal persons. Pooling capital, filing personal tax, listing shares, or enjoying limited liability must be attached to the correct side of that split — limited liability marks incorporated forms, and listing is not the sole criterion of incorporation.

